      "reason": "Archival company history supports the career and business contribution."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>William Lawrence Stephenson was a British retailer who helped establish Woolworth's British business and later became its managing director and chairman. Recruited for his buying experience, he adapted the American variety-store model to British suppliers and shoppers. His leadership helped turn the company into one of the country's largest retail chains.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A visit to American Woolworth stores gave William Lawrence Stephenson an idea for British shoppers: inexpensive gramophone records. His managing director doubted that enough households owned players and permitted only a limited trial. After succeeding him in 1923, Stephenson extended the range across the chain.\n\nTo develop the British offering, he commissioned Crystallate and Vocalion to compete for sales, holding out the prospect of choosing one supplier. The rivals eventually combined resources to invest in electrical recording equipment. Their improved discs appeared under the Victory label. By the end of the decade, Woolworth's weekly record sales approached a quarter of a million.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Detailed museum account explains the product trial, competing suppliers and eventual recorded outcome."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 102,
    "html": "<p>A visit to American Woolworth stores gave William Lawrence Stephenson an idea for British shoppers: inexpensive gramophone records. His managing director doubted that enough households owned players and permitted only a limited trial. After succeeding him in 1923, Stephenson extended the range across the chain.</p>\r\n<p>To develop the British offering, he commissioned Crystallate and Vocalion to compete for sales, holding out the prospect of choosing one supplier. The rivals eventually combined resources to invest in electrical recording equipment. Their improved discs appeared under the Victory label. By the end of the decade, Woolworth's weekly record sales approached a quarter of a million.</p>\r\n"
  },
